"Pets - Big Thicket National Preserve (U.S. National Park Service) (https://www.nps.gov/bith/planyourvisit/pets.htm)\nciteturn10view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.nps.gov/bith/planyourvisit/pets.htm\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 110\nL0: cite0†National Park Service L1: \nL2: Big Thicket National Preserve\nL3: \nL4: Search\nL5: \nL6: [Input: Keyword Search]\nL7: \nL8: [Button: This Site ] [Button: All NPS ]\nL9: \nL10: [Input] [Input]\nL11: \nL12: Menu Close\nL13: \nL14: ## Explore This Park\nL15: \nL16: ## Explore the National Park Service\nL17: \nL18: [Button: Cancel]\nL19: \nL20: cite1†Big Thicket National Preserve L21: \nL22: # Pets\nL23: ## Big Thicket National Preserve is Pet-Friendly!\nL24: \nL25:   cite2†Image: woof! Enjoy some quality time with your four-legged friends on a trail. NPS Photo / Scott Sharaga Pets are welcome on all trails in the preserve. We ask that you follow a few rules when bringing your pet:\nL26:   * Pets must be on-leash at all times (6-foot maximum leash length)\nL27:   * Pick up and dispose of your pet's poop.\nL28:   * Pets are not allowed inside the visitor center or at public programs.\nL29:   * Do not leave your pet unattended outside or inside a vehicle.\nL30:   * Hunting with dogs is not allowed.\nL31: Exception: Retrievers may be used to retrieve waterfowl.\nL32: ## Service Animals\nL33: \nL34: NPS policy defines a cite3†service animal as a dog that has been individually trained to do work or perform tasks for the benefit of an individual with a disability, including a physical, sensory, psychiatric, intellectual, or other mental disability. The tasks performed by the animal must be directly related to the person’s disability. Service animals are allowed anywhere that people are allowed.   cite4†Image: BARK Ranger Bandit B.A.R.K. Ranger Bandit on duty NPS Photo / Scott Sharaga\nL35: ## Become a Big Thicket B.A.R.K. Ranger\nL36: \nL37: Bag your pet's waste.\nL38: Always wear a leash.\nL39: Respect wildlife.\nL40: Know where you can go.\nL41: ### Earn a B.A.R.K. Ranger Certificate\nL42: \nL43: Being a B.A.R.K. ranger is easy! Here's how:\nL44:   1. Hike a trail. Leashed pets are welcome on all cite5†trails in Big Thicket National Preserve.\nL45:   2. Bag your pet's poop. Pick up your pet's poop and throw it away in a trash can at the trailhead.\nL46:   3. Show a park ranger your gear. Go to the cite6†visitor center and show us your leash, empty poop bags, and any other gear you brought for your pet, and a ranger will present your pet with their official B.A.R.K. ranger certificate.\nL47: ## Safety\nL48: \nL49: Is your pet prepared to hike with you in the Big Thicket? Here are some things to keep in mind:\nL50: \nL51:   * Extreme summer heat and humidity can cause dehydration and heat exhaustion in you and your pet. Bring water, snacks, and take breaks!\nL52:   * cite7†Venomous snakes and poisonous plants live here. Use a leash and stay on the trail to keep your pet safe.\nL53:   * Ticks may be present, especially on less-developed trails. Conduct tick checks often.\nL54: ## Visiting National Parks With Your Pet\nL55: \nL56: Want to bring your four-legged friends with you on your national park trip? cite8†Learn more about pets in the national parks cite8†» cite9†Image: hiker with dogs Hiker and her dogs on Village Creek Bridge NPS Photo / Max Harper\nL57: \nL58: Last updated: May 28, 2025\nL59: \nL60: ## Plan Your Visit\nL61: \nL62: ### Contact Info\nL63: \nL64: #### Mailing Address:\nL65: \nL66: 6044 FM 420\nL67: Kountze, TX 77625\nL68: \nL69: #### Phone:\nL70: \nL71: 409-951-6700\nL72: \nL73: #### cite10†Contact Us L74: \nL75: ### Tools\nL76: \nL77:   * cite11†FAQ L78:   * cite12†Site Index L79:   * cite13†Español L80: ### Stay Connected\nL81: \nL82: cite14†Image L83: \nL84: [Input: Keyword Search]\nL85: \nL86:   * [Button: This Site ]\nL87:   * [Button: All NPS ]\nL